    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1526">

                <pb facs="1314" n="1056" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg1056"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2368</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of the Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Daniels</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-01-28">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 28, 1916</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your confidential letter of the 26th instant,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1526-f1"/> enclosing a copy of the statement made by the German
                    Foreign Office, as given to the American Ambassador at Berlin, in relation to
                    the request of the German Government that American warships in the Mediterranean
                    fly the national flag during the day and illuminate ships and flag during the
                        night.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1526-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>In reply, this Department, in view of the reasons furnished in its letter of
                    December 10, 1915, to the Department of State, regrets exceedingly that it is
                    unable to change its attitude in relation to this matter. . . .</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Josephus Daniels</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1526-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1526-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1526-f2" n="2" xml:id="d1526-f2-content">
                <p>The statement referred to, contained in the Ambassador’s telegram No. 3386 of
                    January 24, 1916 (not printed), is a <hi rendition="#italic">résumé</hi> of the
                    note from the German Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg1057">p. 1057</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
